### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to filter integers by their leading digit using string conversion. This tests map between types, startsWith, and awareness of the sign issue.

### How do you filter numbers by leading digit?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;

public class LeadingDigitFilter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> accountNumbers = List.of(
            1001, 2345, 1502, 3001, 1999, 4500, 1100);

        List<String> startsWith1 = accountNumbers.stream()
            .map(String::valueOf)
            .filter(s -> s.startsWith("1"))
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Starting with 1: " + startsWith1);
    }
}
```

```text
Starting with 1: [1001, 1502, 1999, 1100]
```

### How do you get numeric results back?

```java
List<Integer> numericResults = accountNumbers.stream()
    .map(String::valueOf)
    .filter(s -> s.startsWith("1"))
    .map(Integer::parseInt)
    .toList();
```

Or avoid the double conversion by filtering in string form and keeping a reference:

```java
List<Integer> efficient = accountNumbers.stream()
    .filter(n -> String.valueOf(n).startsWith("1"))
    .toList();
```

### How do you handle negative numbers?

```java
List<Integer> mixed = List.of(100, -150, 200, -175);

// Negatives start with '-', not a digit
List<String> withSign = mixed.stream()
    .map(String::valueOf)
    .filter(s -> s.startsWith("1"))
    .toList();
System.out.println(withSign);  // [100] -- -150 excluded!

// Strip sign for absolute value comparison
List<Integer> ignoreSign = mixed.stream()
    .filter(n -> String.valueOf(Math.abs(n)).startsWith("1"))
    .toList();
System.out.println(ignoreSign);  // [100, -150, -175]
```

### What is the numeric alternative without string conversion?

```java
public static int leadingDigit(int n) {
    n = Math.abs(n);
    while (n >= 10) {
        n /= 10;
    }
    return n;
}

List<Integer> numeric = accountNumbers.stream()
    .filter(n -> leadingDigit(n) == 1)
    .toList();
```

This avoids creating a string per element, which matters on hot paths with large lists.

### How is this related to Benford's Law?

In many real-world numeric datasets, the leading digit '1' appears about 30% of the time (not the expected 11%). This is Benford's Law. Filtering by leading digit and counting the distribution can detect anomalies or fabricated data.

```java
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

var distribution = accountNumbers.stream()
    .map(n -> String.valueOf(n).charAt(0))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -> c, Collectors.counting()));

System.out.println("Leading digit distribution: " + distribution);
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using toString() on a potentially null Integer.

```java
// WRONG: NPE if list contains null
Integer val = null;
String s = val.toString();  // NPE!

// CORRECT: String.valueOf handles null
String safe = String.valueOf(val);  // "null"
// But better: filter nulls first
```

Mistake: forgetting that startsWith is case-sensitive for string digits.

```java
// For digits this is not an issue, but for hex:
// "A1".startsWith("a") is false
```

### What about filtering by last digit?

```java
List<Integer> endsWith5 = accountNumbers.stream()
    .filter(n -> String.valueOf(n).endsWith("5"))
    .toList();
// Or numerically: n % 10 == 5
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you count numbers starting with each digit?" Group by leading digit and count.
- "How do you filter by a prefix of two digits?" Use startsWith("12") for numbers starting with 12.
- "How do you validate account number format?" Combine startsWith with length checks and matches.
